ClassicLadder s32 and float pins through real HAL.

Companion to classicladder.0, which covers the bit pins. These types needed
their own case: the float variables (%IF/%QF) were rejected outright by the
variable layer before the engine port, so floatin-NN and floatout-NN were inert
in both directions, and nothing exercised the s32 path (%IW/%QW) end to end.

numeric.clp is written for this test rather than copied from a config, because
no shipped project uses the numeric pins. Its single rung:

  - doubles %IW0 into %QW0                    (s32 in -> s32 out)
  - compares %IW0 > 5 to drive %Q0            (s32 in -> bit out)
  - adds one to %IF0 into %QF0                (float in -> float out)
  - accumulates %IW1 into %W0 while %I0 is closed, and publishes %W0 to %QW1

The accumulator is there to check two things at once: that a numeric OPERATE
respects the power reaching it (it must stop accumulating when %I0 opens), and
that the arithmetic expressions are indexed correctly — the elements refer to
them by number, so a renumbering on load would quietly evaluate the wrong one.
%QW1 trails %W0 by one scan because its OPERATE sits in an earlier column;
that is the evaluation order, not a glitch.

`expected` is generated by gen-expected.sh from the LinuxCNC 2.9 engine, which
also serves to validate the hand-written project: if numeric.clp were malformed,
the reference engine would not produce this trace.
